    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and it passes along with the full build. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` as a computed, persisted field on owner creation. `namesakeCount` = the number of existing owners (before this create) whose `firstName` **and** `lastName` match the new owner, compared case-insensitively.\n\nBecause the test reads the value back via `GET /api/owners/{id}` (a separate request), the count is a snapshot taken at create time and persisted, not recomputed on read.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **New pipeline step** \u2014 `rest/function/owner/AssignNamesakeCount.java`: iterates `ownerRepository.findAll()` and counts case-insensitive first+last name matches, setting it on the built owner. It runs before `SaveOwner`, so the new owner never counts itself.\n\n2. **Pipeline wiring** \u2014 `officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`: inserted `assignNamesakeCount` (under `govern: [ transaction ]`) between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`.\n\n3. **Entity** \u2014 `model/Owner.java`: added `Integer namesakeCount` (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n4. **Persistence schema** \u2014 added `namesake_count` to the `owners` table in all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests, h2, mysql, postgres). `ddl-auto=none` means the column must be declared explicitly.\n\n5. **API contract** \u2014 `openapi.yml`: added the read-only `namesakeCount` property to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it (MapStruct auto-maps it by name on `toOwnerDto`; I added an `ignore` on the request\u2192entity mapping to match the other read-only fields).\n\nThe target test (`coreCountsNamesakes`) and the full `mvnw test` build both pass.",
